I-1 / task 004 -- reproducing the marked-set keystroke drop, with task 003's
input-count instrument read at the moment of failure
================================================================================

Scope: features/kill_delete_undo.feature's @requirement-29-bulk-kill,
@requirement-29-bulk-delete and @requirement-29-batch-undo scenarios, which
Phase 3 (docs/reports/phase3-findings.md) and this run's own notes.md record
as intermittently dropping a keystroke in the k/m/j/m marked-set idiom under
load. This log records every run made while chasing a live reproduction, the
host's nproc and /proc/loadavg at each one, and -- for the run that actually
reproduced -- task 003's own counter read at the moment of failure, so "the
byte never arrived" and "the byte arrived and was not acted on" are
distinguishable rather than guessed.

nproc for every run in this log: 28 (runtime.NumCPU() inside the ci/run.sh
sibling; confirmed also via the sibling's own /proc/cpuinfo).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Natural-load reproduction (no synthetic load needed)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Command shape used (ci/run.sh's sibling does not forward -e, so every
env var is set inside the `sh -c`/`bash -c` string itself):

    ci/run.sh bash -c '
      GOFLAGS=-tags=deckinputcount DECK_INPUT_COUNT_FILE=/tmp/i1-counter \
        DECK_GODOG_TAGS="@requirement-29-bulk-kill" \
        go test -count=1 -run TestFeatures -v ./features/
    '

GOFLAGS=-tags=deckinputcount is picked up transparently by the nested
`go build -o <binary> ./cmd/deck` features/lifecycle_test.go's scenario-lifecycle
Before hook runs for every scenario (it does not build with any -tags of its
own), so every deck client this run launches is compiled with task 003's
counting hook active, with no change to any shared test file.
DECK_INPUT_COUNT_FILE is inherited the same way: startScreenDriver's
`cmd.Env = append(os.Environ(), env...)` (features/pty_driver_test.go:101)
means the value set on the outer `go test` process reaches every deck client
process it starts.

Three attempts, single scenario per attempt, single client "A" per attempt
(so the counter file's cumulative total is unambiguous -- there is nothing
else writing to it):

    attempt 1: exit=0  loadavg="2.23 4.90 5.36 1/4610 732"
    attempt 2: exit=0  loadavg="2.23 4.90 5.36 2/4611 1386"
    attempt 3: exit=1  loadavg="2.83 4.94 5.37 1/4521 4883"   <-- REPRODUCED

Note the 1-minute figure (2.83) is well inside the "whole suite green"
range the planner already measured (1.8-2.6), not anywhere near Phase 3's
documented 6-7 trigger band -- this reproduces on ordinary, unloaded runs of
the isolated scenario, so it is not purely a heavy-load phenomenon (see
section 3 for a second, independent confirmation that it is not scratch-dir-
or fingerprint-specific either).

A companion run tagging all three scenarios together also reproduced on its
very first attempt.

    attempt 1: exit=1 (2 of 3 scenarios failed: bulk-kill, bulk-delete)
      loadavg="4.77 6.64 5.92 7/4566 6770"

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. The failure signature (attempt 3 above, @requirement-29-bulk-kill)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    When deck client "A" sends "k"
    And 100 milliseconds pass
    And deck client "A" sends "m"
    Then deck client "A" screen contains "bk-one running [marked]"   -- PASSED
    When deck client "A" sends "j"
    Then deck client "A" screen contains "> bk-two running"          -- FAILED
      after scenario hook failed: client "A" did not show "> bk-two running"
      within 5s: timed out waiting for frame "> bk-two running":
      context deadline exceeded

The driver's own diagnostic on the hung client (features/pty_driver_test.go's
Stop path) prints the exact input timeline it sent, confirming "j" really was
written to the pty 25.6ms after "m":

      [13:03:52.422] +22.97621ms "k"
      [13:03:52.547] +125.604509ms "m"
      [13:03:52.573] +25.607203ms "j"

...and the frame at the 5s timeout shows the cursor never left bk-one:

      |   bk-two running                 |
      | > bk-one running [marked]        |

Full trimmed transcript (godog pretty output, ANSI stripped, the SIGQUIT
goroutine dump from the hung-client kill elided): see
docs/reports/phase3d-i1-repro-godog-excerpt.log (same commit).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Task 003's counter read at the moment of failure -- the verdict
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A background poller inside the same sibling sampled task 003's counter file
every 10ms for the whole run (docs/reports/phase3d-i1-repro-counter-poll.log,
thinned 3x here for size, full resolution reproducible with the command in
section 1). Correlating counter value against each Send call's own wall-clock
timestamp (recorded by the -v run's own timestamps, joined by seconds-since-
epoch, both captured on the same clock inside the same container):

    event                          count just before   count 300ms later
    sends "k"                            141                  143
    sends "m" (marks bk-one)             142                  143
    sends "j"                            143                  143   <-- flat

...and the counter is STILL 143 six full seconds later, at the moment the
step's own 5s WaitForFrame deadline expires, and stays 143 for the rest of
the poll window. "k" and "m" both advanced the counter by exactly 1 within
one poll tick of being sent (the ordinary, working case); "j" never advanced
it at all, at any point before, during, or after the 5s the assertion waited.

VERDICT: the dropped "j" NEVER ARRIVED at deck's own tea.Model Update loop.
This is a delivery-layer drop -- somewhere in the pty write / kernel tty
line discipline / bubbletea's own PTY reader / task 118's coalescing splitter,
strictly below deck's application code -- not a case of the application
receiving the key and failing to act on it. Any fix belongs in that layer,
not in the marked-set/list-navigation application logic itself.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Synthetic-load attempt (the required negative control)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Before the natural-load reproduction above was found, a dedicated, opt-in Go
test was built to force the issue under deliberately elevated CPU load, in
case natural load never reproduced it: features/i1_repro_test.go
(TestI1KeystrokeDropReproduction, gated on DECK_I1_REPRO=1 so it is not part
of any ordinary `go test ./...` run). It drives its own hand-rolled k/m/j/m
idiom against two plainly-created (not scratch/fingerprint-seeded) sessions,
first at natural load, then under self-spawned "yes > /dev/null" CPU hogs
(PIDs captured at spawn, killed by this test's own code -- never by pattern).

    phase 1 (natural load):   80 attempts, 0 reproductions,  loadavg 1.27-2.70
    phase 2 (synthetic load): 190 attempts, 0 reproductions, loadavg climbed
                               to 30.98 1m / 15.58 5m / 7.43 15m (28 hog
                               processes on 28 cores) -- well past Phase 3's
                               documented 6-7 trigger band, sustained for
                               minutes, and still 0/270 reproductions.

Full run log: see the CI invocation in this file's own commit message; the
summary above is the entire result (t.Log output), no scenario text was
weakened to obtain it, and none was needed since it never reproduced.

Reading sections 1-4 together: raw CPU contention (this section) does not
reproduce the drop at all in 270 tries even at ~4x Phase 3's documented
trigger load, while the REAL scenario's own create-session-dialog/scratch-
directory/fingerprinting path (section 1) reproduces it in 1-3 tries at
ORDINARY load. The trigger condition correlates with something in the real
scenario's own timing shape (dialog typing, tmux/sqlite calls interleaved
with the marked-set keystrokes) rather than with bare CPU starvation. This is
a finding for I-1's continuation, not a claim this task resolves I-1 itself;
task 004's job was reproduction plus the counter's verdict, both delivered
above.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. Reproduction commands (for anyone re-running this)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    # single-scenario, instrumented, loop until it fails:
    ci/run.sh bash -c '
      i=0
      while [ $i -lt 40 ]; do
        i=$((i+1))
        rm -f /tmp/i1-counter
        GOFLAGS=-tags=deckinputcount DECK_INPUT_COUNT_FILE=/tmp/i1-counter \
          DECK_GODOG_TAGS="@requirement-29-bulk-kill" \
          go test -count=1 -run TestFeatures -v ./features/ && continue
        echo "reproduced at attempt $i, loadavg=$(cat /proc/loadavg)"
        break
      done
    '

    # the opt-in synthetic-load driver:
    ci/run.sh sh -c 'DECK_I1_REPRO=1 go test -count=1 -run TestI1KeystrokeDropReproduction -v -timeout 600s ./features/'
